WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.403
Idea. Your school has a rule: no phones in class. Break it, and there is a penalty. Because of the rule and the penalty, most pupils obey. Governments control pollution in the same way.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:30.100
A negative externality is harm to third parties, like pollution. Three controls tackle it. Regulation is a legal rule. A fine is a money penalty for breaking a rule. A pollution permit is legal permission to release a limited amount of pollution.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:42.824
With permits, the total is capped, here at one hundred tonnes. Firm A pollutes only forty tonnes, so it sells ten tonnes of spare permits. Firm B must buy them. Cleaner firms earn money.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:01:00.806
Method. Step one, define the control. Step two, name the externality and who it harms. Step three, explain the mechanism. A ban stops the activity. A fine makes harm costly. Permits cap pollution and reward cleaner firms.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:12.809
Step four, use the case evidence. For Assess, weigh it: rules cost money to monitor and enforce, fines may be too low, and the right limit is hard to set. Then judge.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:30.696
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2024. Kenya has banned single-use plastic bags for over five years. Assess the effectiveness of government regulation when dealing with externalities such as pollution. Nine marks.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:41.198
The externality. Dumped bags cause visual pollution, and livestock that eat them can die. Farmers and the public bear costs they did not choose.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:50.324
The mechanism. Bags are illegal and can be punished, so shops and shoppers switch to alternatives, and far fewer bags are dumped.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:59.882
The evidence. The data says the environment is cleaner, with less danger to livestock. But the problem has not been removed totally.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:13.598
The limits. Some people still use bags illegally. Enforcement costs money, which has an opportunity cost. Success needs public cooperation, and bags are only one source of pollution.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:28.271
Judge. Regulation has been quite effective in Kenya, with a visible clean-up. Long-run success depends on strict enforcement. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:38.594
Common mistake. A common mistake is saying a pollution permit lets a firm pollute as much as it likes. The key is the cap, and the trading.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.383
Another is saying a fine stops pollution, without explaining why.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:56.273
Your turn. Now you try. A city fines drivers two hundred dollars for parking in bus lanes. Explain how the fine may reduce an external cost. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:09.854
Answer. A two hundred dollar fine makes parking in a bus lane costly, so fewer drivers do it. Buses are no longer blocked, so passengers, who are third parties, face fewer delays.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:21.382
Remember: define the control, name the externality, explain how behaviour changes, use the evidence, then weigh and judge. Now try the practice questions.
